  "steps": [
    {
      "text": "The ramen eggs should sit in the marinade for 8 hours or more, so it's best to make them the day before. Boil the eggs (6–9 minutes, depending on how soft you like them).",
      "timerMinutes": 7
    },
    {
      "text": "Cool the eggs in an ice bath. Meanwhile, for the marinade, bring 60 ml soy sauce, the mirin, 60 ml water and the sugar to the boil in a small pot. As soon as it boils, turn the heat down and simmer for another minute.",
      "timerMinutes": 1
    },
    {
      "text": "Let the marinade cool and peel the eggs in the meantime. Carefully place the eggs in a small freezer bag, add the cooled marinade and squeeze out as much air as possible. Seal the bag and leave the eggs to marinate in the fridge."
    },
    {
      "text": "For the sesame paste, toast the sesame seeds and blend until creamy.",
      "timerMinutes": 4
    },
    {
      "text": "For the seasoning paste, mix 4 tbsp sesame paste with the miso, 2 tbsp soy sauce, rice vinegar, chilli oil, gochujang, garlic and ginger. Fry briefly in a pot, deglaze with the chicken stock and oat drink, add the salt and bring to the boil.",
      "timerMinutes": 5
    },
    {
      "text": "Blanch the pak choi.",
      "timerMinutes": 2
    },
    {
      "text": "Cook the noodles according to the package. Cut the spring onions into rings.",
      "timerMinutes": 4
    },
    {
      "text": "Sear the steak briefly over high heat, let it rest and then cut into strips.",
      "timerMinutes": 4
    },
    {
      "text": "Divide the noodles between bowls, pour over the broth and top with the pak choi, spring onions, halved ramen eggs and steak strips."
    }
  ]
